---
import Layout from '../layouts/StandardLayout.astro';
import { currentSite } from '../lib/site';
import planRecord from '../data/orbit/examples/plan-sso.json';
import snapshotRecord from '../data/orbit/examples/project-snapshot-2026-09-14.json';
import reconciliationRecord from '../data/orbit/examples/reconciliation-001.json';
import sliceRecord from '../data/orbit/examples/slice-001.json';

const key = currentSite(import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SITE_KEY);
const plan = JSON.stringify(planRecord, null, 2);
const snapshot = JSON.stringify(snapshotRecord, null, 2);
const reconciliation = JSON.stringify(reconciliationRecord, null, 2);
const slice = JSON.stringify(sliceRecord, null, 2);
const provenance = `{
  "planId": "plan-sso",
  "planRevision": 1,
  "reconciliationId": "reconciliation-001",
  "sliceId": "slice-001"
}`;
const executionReference = `{
  "sliceId": "slice-001",
  "provider": "github",
  "kind": "issue",
  "id": "142",
  "href": "https://example.org/issues/142"
}`;
---
{key === 'orbit' ? <Layout title="Implementation guide"><section class="page wide"><p class="pathbar">~/orbit/implementation</p><p class="eyebrow">ORBIT v0.1 · INFORMATIVE GUIDE</p><h1>Adopt ORBIT without building a platform.</h1><p class="lede">Keep intent durable, compare it with current project facts, and hand an executor one bounded change at a time.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/quick-start">Start with the quick start →</a><a class="text-link" href="/schemas">View schemas</a></div><div class="guide-note"><strong>Scope</strong><p>This guide is informative. It gives implementers a practical starting point. The published specification and schemas remain authoritative.</p><p>ORBIT does not require a particular agent, tracker, repository layout, storage system, dashboard, or executor.</p></div>

<section class="editorial" id="loop"><p class="section-label">THE PRACTICAL LOOP</p><h2>Move one evidence-backed change forward.</h2><p>Keep the Plan stable while observations and reconciliations change. Materialise a new Execution Slice only when current evidence justifies work.</p><div class="orbit-flow" aria-label="ORBIT reconciliation loop"><div class="orbit-step"><b>01 · PLAN</b><p>State durable intent, Outcomes, acceptance criteria, and constraints.</p></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="orbit-step"><b>02 · OBSERVE</b><p>Capture the project and repository facts relevant to the Plan.</p></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="orbit-step"><b>03 · RECONCILE</b><p>Assess criteria with evidence. Record changes, gaps, and unknowns.</p></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="orbit-step"><b>04 · SLICE</b><p>Define the smallest justified change, with explicit inclusions and exclusions.</p></div><span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="orbit-step"><b>05 · EXECUTE</b><p>Apply local policy and hand an execution-ready slice to the executor.</p></div></div><div class="loop-return"><span aria-hidden="true">↩</span><b>OBSERVE AGAIN</b><span>Capture the resulting project state before the next reconciliation.</span></div></section>

<section class="split" id="minimal-set"><div><p class="section-label">MINIMUM ARTEFACT SET</p><h2>Four core record types implement the loop.</h2><ol class="checklist"><li><b>Plan</b><code>Plan</code><br />Durable intent, Outcomes, and acceptance criteria.</li><li><b>Project Snapshot</b><code>ProjectSnapshot</code><br />Observed project and repository state.</li><li><b>Reconciliation</b><code>Reconciliation</code><br />Assessment of the Plan against observed evidence.</li><li><b>Execution Slice</b><code>ExecutionSlice</code><br />The next bounded change justified by that reconciliation.</li></ol></div><div class="guide-note"><strong>Portable records</strong><p>ORBIT defines the records and their normative fields. It does not define where they are stored or which tool produces them.</p></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="storage"><p class="section-label">STORAGE · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>Keep the representation portable.</h2><p>Use repository files, database records, objects in another system, or another representation that preserves the normative fields.</p><div class="split"><div><p class="section-label">EXAMPLE REPOSITORY LAYOUT</p><p>This layout is illustrative. An <code>.orbit/</code> directory is not required.</p></div><pre class="code">.orbit/
  plan.json
  snapshots/
    snapshot-001.json
  reconciliations/
    reconciliation-001.json
  slices/
    slice-001.json</pre></div><p class="meta">Storage is an implementation choice. Keep record identity, references, and provenance intact when records move between systems.</p></section>

<section class="editorial" id="multiple-plans"><p class="section-label">MULTIPLE PLANS</p><h2>One project can contain multiple independent Plans.</h2><p>A Plan may contain one or more Outcomes. Create a separate Plan when the intent, lifecycle, ownership, or planning context is meaningfully independent. Each Plan has its own stable ID and revision history. Reconciliations and Execution Slices reference the Plan they assess or advance.</p><div class="split"><div><p class="section-label">SHARED PROJECT FACTS</p><p>ProjectSnapshot records are independent of Plan intent. A snapshot may be reused across reconciliations when it contains the repository state and observations relevant to each Plan being assessed.</p><p>Capture a new snapshot when relevant project facts change or when a reconciliation needs observations that the existing snapshot does not contain.</p></div><pre class="code">.orbit/
  plans/
    customer-sso/plan.json
    billing/plan.json
  snapshots/
    snapshot-001.json
  reconciliations/
    customer-sso/reconciliation-001.json
  slices/
    customer-sso/slice-001.json</pre></div><div class="guide-note"><strong>Keep identity explicit.</strong><p>Use stable Plan IDs. Keep revision history within each Plan. Every Reconciliation and Execution Slice must identify the Plan revision on which it depends. Where relevant, preserve the ProjectSnapshot ID, Reconciliation ID, and repository revisions.</p></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="handoff"><p class="section-label">EXECUTION HANDOFF · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>Connect ORBIT to the executor you already use.</h2><p>ORBIT stops at the execution boundary. An execution host can render ORBIT records without becoming the source of truth. It can refresh project state, present a reconciliation for review, apply local policy, and dispatch an Execution Slice through an existing execution path.</p><div class="handoff-stack" aria-label="Execution handoff"><div class="handoff-node"><b>ORBIT records</b><span>Plan · ProjectSnapshot · Reconciliation · ExecutionSlice</span></div><div class="handoff-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="handoff-node"><b>Local policy / review</b><span>Review surface · tracker · orchestration layer</span></div><div class="handoff-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="handoff-node"><b>Executor</b><span>Issue · branch · agent · CI · merge mechanics</span></div></div><p class="meta">Provider-specific mechanics belong outside the portable ExecutionSlice contract. The slice carries the objective, scope, constraints, and provenance needed for a safe handoff.</p></section>

<section class="editorial" id="materialisation"><p class="section-label">MATERIALISATION · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>Turn a justified slice into executable work.</h2><p>Materialisation is the act of turning an execution-ready Execution Slice into provider-specific executable work. It is an implementation concern, not a new durable planning layer.</p><pre class="code boundary-code">DURABLE PLANNING                         PROVIDER-SPECIFIC EXECUTION

Plan
  ↓
ProjectSnapshot
  ↓
Reconciliation
  ↓
ExecutionSlice
  │
  │ execution-ready
  ▼
─────────────────── EXECUTION BOUNDARY ─────────────────────
  │
  ▼
Local policy
  │
  ▼
Materialise
  ├── GitHub issue
  ├── Jira ticket
  ├── ApexYard job
  ├── Agent run
  ├── CI workflow
  └── Direct developer handoff</pre><p class="meta">ORBIT ends at the portable Execution Slice. The execution host decides how that slice becomes executable work.</p></section>

<section class="split" id="ticket-boundary"><div><p class="section-label">WHEN DOES WORK BECOME A TICKET?</p><h2>Only after current evidence justifies a slice.</h2><p>Not during planning. Not when an Outcome is created. Not when a possible future task is discovered.</p><p>A ticket may be created only after reconciliation has produced an execution-ready Execution Slice and the selected execution host chooses a ticket-based representation.</p></div><div class="equation-list"><p><b>Plan</b><span>≠ backlog</span></p><p><b>Outcome</b><span>≠ epic</span></p><p><b>Execution Slice</b><span>≠ ticket</span></p><p><b>Ticket</b><span>= optional projection of an Execution Slice</span></p></div></section>

<section class="split" id="ticket-projection"><div><p class="section-label">TICKET MATERIALISATION · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>One provider projection.</h2><p class="meta">ORBIT Execution Slice · <code>slice-001</code></p><p><b>Objective</b><br />Configure the existing provider for the corporate IdP.</p><p><b>Include</b><br />IdP mapping<br />Login regression test</p><p><b>Exclude</b><br />Provider replacement<br />Account migration</p><p><b>Based on</b><br /><code>plan-sso</code> rev 1 · <code>reconciliation-001</code> · <code>web @ 81fee032</code></p></div><div><p class="section-label">↓ GITHUB ADAPTER MATERIALISES</p><div class="artefact"><p><b># Configure corporate IdP mapping</b></p><p><b>Objective</b><br />Configure the existing authentication provider for the corporate IdP.</p><p><b>Scope</b><br />Add IdP mapping<br />Add login regression test</p><p><b>Out of scope</b><br />Provider replacement<br />Account migration</p><p><b>ORBIT provenance</b><br />Plan: <code>plan-sso @ revision 1</code><br />Reconciliation: <code>reconciliation-001</code><br />Slice: <code>slice-001</code><br />Repository: <code>web @ 81fee032</code></p></div><p class="meta">The issue is an execution projection. The Execution Slice remains the portable source of execution intent.</p></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="execution-strategies"><p class="section-label">OPTIONAL TICKET PROJECTION</p><h2>The same slice can be executed differently.</h2><pre class="code boundary-code">                   ExecutionSlice
                         │
         ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
         │               │               │
         ▼               ▼               ▼
    GitHub issue      Agent run      CI workflow
         │               │               │
         ▼               ▼               ▼
      execute          execute          execute</pre><p>ORBIT does not require work to become a ticket. Ticket creation is one possible execution strategy.</p></section>

<section class="split" id="provenance"><div><p class="section-label">MATERIALISATION PROVENANCE</p><h2>Keep the origin traceable.</h2><p>Provider-specific work created from a slice should preserve enough provenance to identify its ORBIT origin.</p><pre class="code">{provenance}</pre><p class="meta">Where relevant, also preserve the ProjectSnapshot ID, repository IDs and revisions, and attachment hashes.</p></div><div><p class="section-label">EXECUTION REFERENCE · INFORMATIVE</p><p>An ExecutionReference is a useful host-side record linking a slice to materialised work. It is not required to be part of the portable ORBIT core.</p><pre class="code">{executionReference}</pre></div></section>

<section class="split" id="idempotency"><div><p class="section-label">MATERIALISATION SHOULD BE IDEMPOTENT</p><h2>Reuse an existing execution reference.</h2><p>An execution host should not create duplicate work for the same Execution Slice. If <code>slice-001</code> has already been materialised, return or reuse the existing execution reference.</p></div><pre class="code boundary-code">slice-001
    ↓
lookup execution reference
    │
    ├── exists → reuse
    │
    └── absent → materialise</pre></section>

<section class="split" id="examples"><div><p class="section-label">PLAN · <code>Plan</code></p><p>Keep durable intent stable while implementation details change.</p><pre class="code">{plan}</pre></div><div><p class="section-label">PROJECT SNAPSHOT · <code>ProjectSnapshot</code></p><p>Capture the state used for the next assessment.</p><pre class="code">{snapshot}</pre></div><div><p class="section-label">RECONCILIATION · <code>Reconciliation</code></p><p>Explain the assessment and preserve structured evidence.</p><pre class="code">{reconciliation}</pre></div><div><p class="section-label">EXECUTION SLICE · <code>ExecutionSlice</code></p><p>Make scope, exclusions, and provenance visible before dispatch.</p><pre class="code">{slice}</pre></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="closed-loop"><p class="section-label">CLOSED LOOP · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>Execution changes the next observation.</h2><pre class="code boundary-code">                       durable
                         Plan
                          │
                          ▼
                   ProjectSnapshot
                          │
                          ▼
                   Reconciliation
                          │
                          ▼
                   ExecutionSlice
                          │
                   execution boundary
                          │
                          ▼
                    Materialise
                   /     |      \
                  /      |       \
              ticket   agent     CI
                  \      |       /
                   \     |      /
                        execute
                          │
                          ▼
                   project changes
                          │
                          └─────────────┐
                                        │
                                        ▼
                                 observe again</pre></section>

<section class="split" id="durable-derived"><div><p class="section-label">DURABLE VS DERIVED</p><h2>Durable intent survives execution cycles.</h2><pre class="code boundary-code">DURABLE

Plan
├── Intent
├── Outcomes
├── Acceptance criteria
└── Constraints

            ↓ reconciliation

CURRENT / DERIVED

ProjectSnapshot
Reconciliation
ExecutionSlice
Ticket / Job / Agent run</pre></div><div class="guide-note"><strong>Derived work may be replaced.</strong><p>Project reality changes. A new reconciliation may justify a different slice or no slice at all. Provider work items do not become the durable planning source of truth.</p></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="changes"><p class="section-label">WHAT CHANGES WHEN?</p><h2>Keep each record's trigger clear.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table class="small-table"><thead><tr><th>Artefact</th><th>Changes when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Plan</code></td><td>Intent or planning knowledge changes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ProjectSnapshot</code></td><td>Relevant project reality changes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Reconciliation</code></td><td>The Plan is reassessed against a snapshot.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ExecutionSlice</code></td><td>New work is justified by the reconciliation.</td></tr><tr><td>Ticket / job</td><td>A host materialises a slice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="split" id="materialisation-timing"><div><p class="section-label">DO NOT PRE-MATERIALISE</p><h2>Materialise only the work justified now.</h2><pre class="code boundary-code">Plan
  ↓
reconcile
  ↓
slice-001
  ↓
execute
  ↓
observe
  ↓
reconcile
  ↓
slice-002</pre></div><div><p class="section-label">SPECULATIVE FUTURE WORK</p><p>ORBIT may identify likely future work, but it should not automatically materialise that work as executable tasks.</p><p>Likely future work may be recorded as discovery, captured as planning context, represented as unresolved Outcome work, or deferred.</p><p class="meta">It should not be automatically turned into tickets.</p></div></section>

<section class="split" id="outcome-projection"><div><p class="section-label">OUTCOME PROJECTION · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>An Outcome is not a ticket container.</h2><p>An Outcome is a durable desired result. It is not an epic, milestone, ticket group, or issue hierarchy. An implementation may project an Outcome to a provider parent issue, but that projection must not change the meaning of the Outcome.</p></div><pre class="code boundary-code">ORBIT

Plan
└── Outcome: Corporate SSO
      │
      ├── Reconciliation
      │
      └── ExecutionSlice slice-001

                    ↓ provider projection

GitHub

Parent issue: Corporate SSO
└── Issue #142: Configure corporate IdP mapping

The hierarchy is a projection. ORBIT does not depend on it.</pre></section>

<section class="split" id="safety"><div><p class="section-label">TYPICAL LOOP TRANSITIONS · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>Apply safety rules at the boundary.</h2><p>These are implementation events and recommended actions. They are not a normative executor state machine.</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table class="small-table"><thead><tr><th>Event or condition</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Recommended action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plan revision changed</td><td>Durable intent or criteria changed.</td><td>Capture a new snapshot and reconcile again.</td></tr><tr><td>Relevant project state changed</td><td>The facts used by a reconciliation may no longer describe the project.</td><td>Capture a new snapshot before producing a slice.</td></tr><tr><td>Slice eligible for execution</td><td>Current evidence supports the bounded scope.</td><td>Apply local policy and dispatch.</td></tr><tr><td>Execution completed</td><td>The executor produced code, evidence, or a recorded result.</td><td>Observe the resulting state and reconcile again.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="split stale-section" id="stale"><div><p class="section-label">STALE WORK</p><h2>Stop when provenance changes.</h2><p>An Execution Slice is justified against specific planning and project state. If a relevant Plan revision or repository revision changes before execution, the slice is stale.</p><div class="guide-note"><strong>Do not silently rebase or dispatch a stale slice.</strong><p>Observe the new state, reconcile the Plan again, and produce a new slice.</p></div></div><div class="stale-flow"><div class="stale-node"><b>slice-001</b><span>based on <code>web @ 81fee032</code></span></div><div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="stale-node"><b>Repository changes</b><span><code>web @ a9127bf0</code></span></div><div class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</div><div class="stale-node stale-warning"><b>STALE</b><span>Observe → Reconcile → new slice</span></div></div></section>

<section class="split" id="discoveries"><div><p class="section-label">DISCOVERIES</p><h2>A discovery is planning input, not automatically new work.</h2><p>Only required discoveries should independently create additional work for the active Outcome.</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table class="small-table"><thead><tr><th>Classification</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Required</td><td>May justify work needed to achieve the active Outcome.</td></tr><tr><td>Supporting</td><td>May shape current work or reconciliation, but does not independently justify a separate slice.</td></tr><tr><td>Adjacent</td><td>Record and defer by default.</td></tr><tr><td>Unrelated</td><td>Record only when useful. Do not expand the active Outcome.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

<section class="split" id="adopter-checklist"><div><p class="section-label">ADOPTION CHECKLIST</p><h2>Start small.</h2><ol class="checklist"><li>Choose one meaningful Outcome.</li><li>Create one Plan with stable acceptance-criterion IDs.</li><li>Capture the relevant project state.</li><li>Reconcile the Plan against observed evidence.</li><li>Generate one bounded Execution Slice with explicit inclusions and exclusions.</li><li>Apply an explicit execution policy.</li><li>Dispatch the slice.</li><li>Observe the result and reconcile again.</li></ol></div><div><p class="section-label">INITIAL POLICY</p><h2>Choose the control that fits.</h2><p>ORBIT supports human approval, automated policy, trusted autonomous execution, and organisation-specific governance.</p><div class="guide-note"><strong>Recommended for initial adoption.</strong><p>Human approval is a simple starting policy. It is an implementation choice, not an ORBIT requirement.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="roles"><p class="section-label">IMPLEMENTATION ROLES · INFORMATIVE</p><h2>Adopt capabilities incrementally.</h2><p>These labels describe implementation responsibilities. They are informative unless the specification defines them as formal conformance classes.</p><div class="role-grid"><p><b>Producer</b><br />Emits one or more valid ORBIT records.</p><p><b>Observer</b><br />Produces factual ProjectSnapshot records from project state.</p><p><b>Reconciler</b><br />Compares a Plan with observed state and produces a Reconciliation.</p></div><div class="role-grid"><p><b>Executor</b><br />Accepts an execution-ready ExecutionSlice and attempts the bounded change.</p><p><b>Consumer</b><br />Reads ORBIT records without owning their storage or production.</p><p><b>Resolver</b><br />Optionally resolves external references used by ORBIT records.</p></div></section>

<section class="split" id="boundaries"><div><p class="section-label">EXECUTOR NEUTRAL</p><h2>ORBIT does not define how code is changed.</h2><p>An Execution Slice may be consumed by a coding agent, developer, CI workflow, issue tracker integration, orchestration system, or another execution mechanism. Executor-specific details must not become required fields in the portable Plan.</p><p class="meta">Execution Slices are derived work, not the durable planning source of truth. ORBIT does not require a complete future task list.</p></div><div><p class="section-label">ORBIT DOES NOT DEFINE</p><ul class="checklist"><li>Issue trackers</li><li>Branch or pull-request workflows</li><li>Agent runtimes or task queues</li><li>Dashboards or repository storage layout</li><li>Approval systems or CI providers</li></ul><p>ORBIT defines portable planning and reconciliation artefacts. Implementations decide how those artefacts are stored, reviewed, and executed.</p></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="mental-model"><p class="section-label">FINAL MENTAL MODEL</p><h2>Keep the boundary clear.</h2><div class="rows"><p><b>Plan</b><span>tells us what outcome remains durable.</span></p><p><b>ProjectSnapshot</b><span>tells us what is true now.</span></p><p><b>Reconciliation</b><span>tells us what the evidence means.</span></p><p><b>ExecutionSlice</b><span>tells us what bounded change is justified now.</span></p><p><b>Materialisation</b><span>turns that slice into provider-specific executable work.</span></p><p><b>Ticket</b><span>is one possible materialisation.</span></p></div><div class="guide-note"><strong>Tickets are projections of justified work, not the planning source of truth.</strong></div></section>

<section class="editorial" id="next"><p class="section-label">NEXT</p><h2>Start with one loop.</h2><p>Create one Plan. Observe the current project state. Reconcile once. Produce one bounded Execution Slice. Execute it. Observe again.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/quick-start">Quick Start →</a><a class="text-link" href="/schemas">View the schemas</a><a class="text-link" href="/specification">Read the specification</a></div></section>
